- [x] The problem can be successfully reproduced in the latest Release

### Description

Version: Xray-core 26.10.10 (701af60, go1.27.2), hysteria outbound. Server: Hysteria v2.11.0. Also seen with Xray as a library on Android.

When the target of a proxied upload stops reading while keeping its connection open (an upload speed-test server does this once it has measured enough), the Hysteria server stops reading that QUIC stream. The stream's unread data fills the QUIC connection-level flow-control window (20 MB by default; a single stream can hold up to 8 MB), and the outbound's writer for that stream blocks in `stream.Write`.

When the local connection then ends (the app aborts the upload with a reset), the blocked writer never notices, so `interConn.Close()` (`CancelRead` + `Close`) is only reached when the session is torn down by the `connIdle` timeout. Until then the stream keeps its share of the connection window. With 3–4 such streams the whole connection stalls: every new TCP request over the hysteria outbound hangs, while UDP over datagrams keeps working. On Android we saw it as Hysteria going dead about a minute into an upload speed test.

With an instrumented server, no STOP_SENDING / reset arrived from the client for the stalled streams until the Xray process was killed.

Suggestion: when a session ends while the outbound's write is blocked, cancel the stream's write side (`CancelWrite`, i.e. RESET_STREAM) so the blocked `Write` returns and the server can release the stream's flow-control credit. A server-side mitigation is proposed in HyNetworks/hysteria#1702 / HyNetworks/hysteria#1703; a client-side fix would help with servers that do not have it.

### Reproduction Method

Everything on loopback, configs below.

1. Run the Hysteria server (`hysteria server -c server.yaml`) and Xray (`xray run -c client.json`).
2. Start a TCP sink on 127.0.0.1:19000 that reads about 2 MB from each connection and then stops reading without closing it, and a health target on 127.0.0.1:19001 that answers `OK` and closes.
3. Through the SOCKS inbound, open 4 connections to 127.0.0.1:19000 and write as fast as possible for 4 s, then close them with a reset (SO_LINGER 0).
4. Through the SOCKS inbound, connect to 127.0.0.1:19001: the SOCKS handshake completes but no data ever comes back. In our run 0 of 5 attempts succeeded within 30 s, and none after another 10 s idle.

With a Hysteria server that closes stalled targets (the PR above) the same steps recover in about 8 s.

